WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.740
Why can't a young songbird catch up on a song it never heard?

2
00:00:07.783 --> 00:00:11.723
A male white-crowned sparrow raised without adult song still sings.

3
00:00:12.523 --> 00:00:14.263
But its song is simple and abnormal.

4
00:00:15.367 --> 00:00:18.267
The right song played later does little to fix it.

5
00:00:18.927 --> 00:00:21.027
Yet the bird is not deaf, and its voice works.

6
00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:24.970
It missed an experience at the right age.

7
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.900
Peter Marler asked when it must come.

8
00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:31.420
He raised young sparrows in sound-isolated chambers.

9
00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:34.970
He played them recorded song at chosen ages.

10
00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:38.430
Then he waited, and recorded what they sang as adults.

11
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:43.960
Predict. Some birds heard the tape early, some later, some never.

12
00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:46.040
Which ones sing a good copy?

13
00:00:52.533 --> 00:00:57.113
Birds that heard song early sang good copies, though months passed before they first

14
00:00:57.113 --> 00:00:58.113
sang.

15
00:00:58.433 --> 00:01:00.893
Tapes played to older birds had little effect.

16
00:01:01.973 --> 00:01:05.133
Birds that heard no adult song sang simple, abnormal songs.

17
00:01:06.183 --> 00:01:09.483
The same tape, at different ages, left very different marks.

18
00:01:13.417 --> 00:01:16.117
Marler's result splits learning into two phases.

19
00:01:17.097 --> 00:01:20.297
First, a sensory phase: the bird listens and memorizes.

20
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:26.620
Then a sensorimotor phase: it practices, shaping rambling sounds until they match

21
00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:27.620
the memory.

22
00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:29.550
What does practice need?

23
00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:35.050
Masakazu Konishi deafened young sparrows after they heard a tutor, but before they

24
00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:36.050
practiced.

25
00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:41.530
Their songs were extremely abnormal, no better than those of birds deafened before

26
00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:42.530
ever hearing a tutor.

27
00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:48.380
So a bird must hear itself, to match its practice to a stored memory: a template.

28
00:01:51.833 --> 00:01:53.253
That defines a window.

29
00:01:54.113 --> 00:01:57.113
Plot what lasts against the age at which the input was given.

30
00:01:58.133 --> 00:02:02.473
This course calls a window that ends abruptly a critical period, like a door.

31
00:02:03.933 --> 00:02:08.333
A window that fades gradually it calls a sensitive period, like a tide.

32
00:02:09.653 --> 00:02:11.793
Researchers also use these terms in other ways.

33
00:02:12.733 --> 00:02:17.353
The clearest human case here, second-language grammar, is a tide in the outcome.

34
00:02:18.313 --> 00:02:19.913
Telling them apart takes the whole curve.

35
00:02:21.117 --> 00:02:23.237
So here is the one idea to carry.

36
00:02:24.157 --> 00:02:26.617
Same input, different ages: measure what lasts.

37
00:02:27.667 --> 00:02:32.267
In zebra finches, the expected input itself seems to help end the window.

38
00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:38.420
A window is a range of ages when the same experience leaves an unusually strong,

39
00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:39.380
lasting mark.
